<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="leucopelargonidin and leucocyanidin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY1F-823" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Leucoanthocyanidins such as leucopelargonidin and leucocyanidin are colorless intermediates in the biosynthetic course to build up the corresponding colored anthocyanins (|FRAME:PWY-5125|) |CITS: [12828368]|. Leucoanthocyanidins are compounds at a branch-point embedded in that metabolic sequence. They either can be oxidized to form anthocyanins or can be reduced to serve as precursors for catechins and proanthocyanins (|FRAME:PWY-641|) |CITS: [12435592]| |CITS: [15720617]|. As such leucoanthocyanidins represent an important regulatory point to feed a range of flavonoid subclasses of diverse biological properties |CITS: [11402179]|. Flavonoids are extensively modified by a number of reactions such as hydroxylation, methylation, glycosylation and acylation. The hydroxylation of the flavonoid B-ring at the 3&apos;,4&apos; or 5&apos; position leads to a variety of flavonoids contributing to different colors of flowers and seeds |CITS: [11287230]|. The monohydroxylated leucopelargonidin and the dihydroxylated leucocyanidin give rise to anthocyanins adding red/pink colors to a broad variety of plants, including &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [10504566]| |CITS: [10536025]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Leucopelargonidin and leucocyanidin derive from dihydrokaempferol and dihydroquercetin, respectively which, in turn are either formed by a Fe&lt;sup&gt;2+&lt;/sup&gt;/oxoglutarate-dependent hydroxylation of naringenin to generate dihydrokaempferol and eriodictyol to make dihydroquercetin |CITS: [8223617]| |CITS: [8685272]| or a cytochrome P450-dependent monooxygenase (flavonoid 3&apos;hydroxylase) which hydroxylates dihydrokaempferol to form dihydroquercetin |CITS: [11030432]|. The discernible step in the biosynthetic route towards leucoanthocyanidins is the stereospecific reduction of 2R,3R)-dihydroflavonols carried out by dihydroflavonol 4-reductase. The enzyme exhibits a pronounced substrate specificity which determines a visible pattern of anthocyanins, catechins and proanthocyanidins indicative for certain species |CITS: [12435592]|. The reduction of |FRAME:DIHYDROKAEMPFEROL-CMPD| and |FRAME:CPD-474| to form the respective leucoanthocyanins is considered the rate-limiting step in the pathway |CITS:[22363429]|. However, the dihyfroflavonol 4-reductase (DFR) catalyzed reaction is at an branch-point for both anthocyanin and condensed tannins biosynthesis (compare |FRAME:PWY-641|). The finding that isoenzymes of DFR within the same species, e.g. &lt;i&gt;Ginkgo biloba&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[23991027]|, &lt;i&gt;Populus trichocarpa&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[22363429]| and &lt;i&gt;Populus tremuloides&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[12472686]| are rather involved in condensed tannins synthesis than in anthocyanin accumulation indicates a functional shift of the enzyme that supports the systemic herbivore response in wooden species |CITS:[12472686]|. The lack of the B-ring modifying enzyme flavonoid 3&apos;,5&apos;-hydroxylase catalyzing the formation of 3&apos;,4&apos;,5&apos;-pentahydroxylated compounds and derivatives thereof (e.g. dihydromyricetin) in Arabidopsis |CITS: [10536025]| prevents the biosynthesis of the glucosylated anthocyanidins delphinidin, petunidin and malvidin, contributing to the blue/violet colors in plants (compare |FRAME:PWY-5152|).</Description>
